  [1mFirst question — how should the environment actually get selected?[0m

  The fork: whether switching from dev to prod is something a [3mperson[0m does by editing a file, something the [3mbrowser[0m
  decides at runtime from where the page is served, or something a [3mbuild step[0m bakes in. This is the choice that
  determines everything else, because it dictates whether this project acquires a build pipeline it currently doesn't
  have.

  - [1mHand-edit one constant[0m — buys the smallest possible change: one new file, one exported value, zero new tooling.
    Costs a manual edit (and a real risk of committing the wrong endpoint) at every environment switch. Rules out
    nothing; you can add detection or a build later without changing how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m consumes it.
  - [1mRuntime detection from hostname[0m — buys automatic switching with still no build step: the settings module maps
    [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m → dev, everything else → prod. Costs a hostname-to-environment table you have to keep correct, and all
    endpoints ship in the client bundle (fine here — these are public URLs, not secrets). Cheap to change later.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m — buys the conventional [38;5;153m.env[39m + bundler workflow and the only option where non-prod URLs never
    reach production. Costs introducing a bundler, a dependency tree, and a build step to a project that today is opened
    as a static file. Expensive to undo.

  My recommendation is [1mruntime detection from hostname[0m. It delivers the actual goal in your request — "easier to change
  environments" means not editing code to switch — without dragging a build pipeline into a static three-file webapp.
  The consuming interface ([38;5;153mapp.js[39m reads a settings object) is identical under all three, so if you later outgrow it,
  build-time injection replaces the module's internals without touching callers.
